   "source": [
    "{\n",
    " \"cells\": [\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c00\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# CNMFe Tutorial — realistic miniscope movie\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Same structure as `tutorial2.ipynb`, but the synthetic dataset is generated by `tests/miniscope_simulator.py::make_miniscope_movie` instead of the clean fixture in `conftest.py`. The realistic generator adds the messy features you actually see on a 1p endoscope:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- heterogeneous neuron sizes / shapes / orientations\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- per-neuron AR(1) decay and per-spike amplitudes (with bursts)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- multi-component drifting background at several spatial / temporal scales\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- a strong, temporally-**active neuropil** — the defining 1p feature: it makes nearly every pixel correlate with its neighbours (high CORR everywhere) and is the main source of spurious detections\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **ghost cells** — large out-of-focus blurry blobs\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- out-of-focus haloes around each cell; neurons are ~σ5 px\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- vasculature pulsation at heart rate + slow drift\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- radial vignetting\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- photobleaching\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- photon shot noise (Poisson) + read noise (Gaussian)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- 8-bit camera quantisation\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"We walk through the same stages — motion correction, CORR/PNR, ring background, greedy init, refinement, full pipeline — and end with the same trace-flavour comparisons as `tutorial2.ipynb`. A single `difficulty` knob (0 = a clean operating point calibrated to a real recording, 1 = hard/low-SNR) and a `realism=False` kill-switch dial the regime. Expect **more detections than truth** (neuropil/ghost seeds) and lower r than the clean tutorial - that's realistic 1p extraction.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c01\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:51.335094188Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:51.169701761Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": \"import sys, os, tempfile, time\\n\\n# Make the repo root importable whether this notebook is launched from the repo\\n# root or from demo_notebooks/ (so `import cnmfe` / `import tests` both work).\\n_here = os.path.abspath(\\\".\\\")\\nfor _cand in (_here, os.path.dirname(_here), os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(_here))):\\n    if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(_cand, \\\"cnmfe\\\")) and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(_cand, \\\"tests\\\")):\\n        if _cand not in sys.path:\\n            sys.path.insert(0, _cand)\\n        break\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport scipy.ndimage as ndi\\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\n%matplotlib inline\\nplt.rcParams['figure.dpi'] = 120\",\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 1\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c02\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 1. Generate a realistic miniscope movie\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"`A_true` / `C_true` hold **only the true foreground neurons** (their in-focus cores). Every nuisance source - the active neuropil, out-of-focus haloes, ghost cells, vasculature, slow drift - lives in `data['background']`, which is exactly the structure a real CNMFe run has to attribute to background rather than to a cell. The active neuropil in particular makes this a genuinely hard, real-data-like demixing problem.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c03\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:54.743385783Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:52.579606240Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": \"from tests.miniscope_simulator import make_miniscope_movie\\n\\n# Realistic GCaMP8m miniscope movie. The simulator is calibrated (difficulty=0)\\n# so its CORR/PNR match a real *clean* recording: a strong, temporally-active\\n# neuropil makes nearly every pixel correlated with its neighbours, neuron\\n# transients are a small ΔF on a large uint8-like baseline, plus ghost cells,\\n# vasculature, vignetting, photobleaching and Poisson+read noise.\\n#\\n# Difficulty knob: difficulty=0 (here) is the clean operating point; raise it\\n# toward 1.0 for a harder, lower-SNR, neuropil-heavier movie, or pass\\n# realism=False for the old low-DC fixture.\\ndata = make_miniscope_movie(\\n    n_neurons=20,\\n    dims=(128, 128),\\n    T=500,\\n    fps=20.0,\\n    decay_time_ms=180.0,   # GCaMP8m indicator decay\\n    n_ghost_cells=6,       # out-of-focus false-positive sources\\n    difficulty=0.0,\\n    seed=1,\\n)\\n\\nmovie    = data['movie']        # (T, H, W) float32\\nA_true   = data['A_true']       # (H*W, K) — true neuron CORES only (no neuropil/ghosts)\\nC_true   = data['C_true']       # (K, T)\\nS_true   = data['S_true']\\ncenters  = data['centers']\\ng_true   = data['g_true']       # per-neuron AR(1) decay (GCaMP8m at this fps)\\nT, H, W  = movie.shape\\nK        = C_true.shape[0]\\n\\nprint(f'Movie shape:        {movie.shape}')\\nprint(f'True neurons:       {K}  (neuropil, ghosts, vasculature all live in the background)')\\nprint(f'Pixel intensity:    median {np.median(movie):.0f}, range [{movie.min():.0f}, {movie.max():.0f}]  (uint8-like)')\\nprint(f'AR(1) g (GCaMP8m):  {g_true.min():.3f} - {g_true.max():.3f}')\",\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"name\": \"stdout\",\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"stream\",\n",
    "     \"text\": [\n",
    "      \"Movie shape:        (500, 128, 128)\\n\",\n",
    "      \"True neurons:       20  (neuropil, ghosts, vasculature all live in the background)\\n\",\n",
    "      \"Pixel intensity:    median 28, range [0, 71]  (uint8-like)\\n\",\n",
    "      \"AR(1) g (GCaMP8m):  0.735 - 0.774\\n\"\n",
    "     ]\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 2\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c04\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### What's making this movie hard?\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Before running anything we visualise a few of the artefacts. These are pre-loaded into `data` so the tutorial can show them directly.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c05\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:55.548703220Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:55.286947860Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# --- Photobleaching: overall fluorescence drops over time ------------------\\n\",\n",
    "    \"mean_per_frame = movie.reshape(T, -1).mean(axis=1)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].plot(mean_per_frame, lw=0.8, label='mean intensity')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].plot(mean_per_frame[0] * data['bleach'], lw=1.2, ls='--', color='k', alpha=0.6,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"             label='bleach curve (×scale)')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].set_xlabel('Frame'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Pixel intensity (mean)')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].set_title('Photobleaching — total brightness drifts down')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].legend(fontsize=8)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# --- Vignetting: corner pixels are dimmer than centre ----------------------\\n\",\n",
    "    \"im = axes[1].imshow(data['vignette'], cmap='viridis')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].set_title('Vignette mask (multiplicative)')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].axis('off')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.colorbar(im, ax=axes[1], fraction=0.046)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"data\": {\n",
    "      \"text/plain\": [\n",
    "       \"<Figure size 1440x360 with 3 Axes>\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"image/png\": \"\"\n",
    "     },\n",
    "     \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n",
    "     \"jetTransient\": {\n",
    "      \"display_id\": null\n",
    "     }\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 3\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c06\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:57.201353837Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:08:56.885252677Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# --- A raw frame, the true footprints, and a trace from a single pixel -----\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(13, 4))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].imshow(movie[100], cmap='gray',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"               vmin=np.percentile(movie, 1), vmax=np.percentile(movie, 99))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[0].set_title('Raw frame'); axes[0].axis('off')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].imshow(A_true.max(axis=1).reshape(H, W), cmap='hot')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"for r, c in centers:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    axes[1].plot(c, r, 'w+', ms=10, mew=2)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[1].set_title(f'True footprints (n={K})'); axes[1].axis('off')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Mean projection — shows vignette, ghost cells, vasculature\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[2].imshow(movie.mean(axis=0), cmap='gray')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[2].set_title('Mean projection\\\\n(vignette + ghost cells + vasculature visible)')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"axes[2].axis('off')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"data\": {\n",
    "      \"text/plain\": [\n",
    "       \"<Figure size 1560x480 with 3 Axes>\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"image/png\": \"\"\n",
    "     },\n",
    "     \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n",
    "     \"jetTransient\": {\n",
    "      \"display_id\": null\n",
    "     }\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 4\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c07\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:00.213320843Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:00.073120599Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# --- A pixel right on a neuron centre vs the underlying calcium trace -----\\n\",\n",
    "    \"r0, c0 = centers[0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"raw_pixel = movie[:, r0, c0]\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 2.5))\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.plot(raw_pixel, lw=0.7, color='gray',\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        label=f'raw pixel ({r0},{c0}) — bleach + bg + vasc + neuron 0')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.plot(C_true[0] * raw_pixel.std() / (C_true[0].std() + 1e-10) + raw_pixel.mean(),\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        lw=1.0, color='steelblue', label='true neuron 0 trace (rescaled to plot)')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.set_xlabel('Frame')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.legend(fontsize=8)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"ax.set_title('On a real 1p pixel the calcium signal is dominated by background drift / bleaching')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"data\": {\n",
    "      \"text/plain\": [\n",
    "       \"<Figure size 1320x300 with 1 Axes>\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"image/png\": \"\"\n",
    "     },\n",
    "     \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"display_data\",\n",
    "     \"jetTransient\": {\n",
    "      \"display_id\": null\n",
    "     }\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 5\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"3444dc62\",\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"# --- Save a normalised mp4 of the simulated movie -------------------------\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# Open this file in any video player and judge whether the simulator looks\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# 1p-realistic. Things to spot while watching:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#   - real neurons         : compact, bright, sharp transients on top of bg\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#   - ghost cells          : large blurry blobs that brighten/dim slowly\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#   - vasculature          : faint elongated lines pulsing at heart rate\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#   - vignetting           : corners persistently darker than centre\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#   - photobleaching       : whole frame slowly dimming over the recording\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#   - shot noise + 8-bit   : grainy, quantised camera-like texture\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# We do NOT per-frame-normalise — that would hide the bleach drift, which is\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# one of the features you want to see.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# We try several writers in order; mp4 is preferred but we fall back to a\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# multi-frame TIFF (always works, viewable in ImageJ / FIJI / napari) if no\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# ffmpeg is reachable from the kernel.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"fps_play  = 20         # playback fps (close to acquisition rate)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"scale     = 4          # upscale factor (128 -> 512 px)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"vmin_pct  = 1\\n\",\n",
    "    \"vmax_pct  = 99.5\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"lo, hi = np.percentile(movie, [vmin_pct, vmax_pct])\\n\",\n",
    "    \"frames_u8 = ((movie - lo) / (hi - lo + 1e-10) * 255).clip(0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if scale > 1:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    frames_u8 = frames_u8.repeat(scale, axis=1).repeat(scale, axis=2)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"T_, Hs, Ws = frames_u8.shape\\n\",\n",
    "    \"out_path = None\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# 1) Try OpenCV (cv2) — most robust on Windows, codec mp4v ships with the\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#    opencv-python wheel.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"try:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    import cv2\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    out_path = '../miniscope_movie.mp4'\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    fourcc = cv2.VideoWriter_fourcc(*'mp4v')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    writer = cv2.VideoWriter(out_path, fourcc, fps_play, (Ws, Hs), isColor=False)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    if not writer.isOpened():\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        raise RuntimeError('cv2.VideoWriter failed to open')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    for f in frames_u8:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        writer.write(f)               # gray-scale uint8 frame\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    writer.release()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    print(f'[cv2/mp4v] saved -> {os.path.abspath(out_path)}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"except Exception as e:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    print(f'cv2 path failed: {e}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    out_path = None\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# 2) Fallback: imageio with explicit codec via the pyav plugin.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if out_path is None:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    try:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        import imageio.v2 as imageio2\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        out_path = '../miniscope_movie.mp4'\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        # libx264 is available in PyAV when ffmpeg/libav is built with x264.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        imageio2.mimwrite(out_path, frames_u8, fps=fps_play,\\n\",\n",
    "    \"                          codec='libx264', macro_block_size=1)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print(f'[imageio/libx264] saved -> {os.path.abspath(out_path)}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    except Exception as e:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print(f'imageio path failed: {e}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        out_path = None\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"# 3) Last-resort fallback: write a multi-frame TIFF. Open in ImageJ / FIJI /\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#    napari to scrub through frames — no codec issues on any platform.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if out_path is None:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    try:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        import tifffile\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        out_path = 'miniscope_movie.tif'\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        tifffile.imwrite(out_path, frames_u8, photometric='minisblack')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print(f'[tifffile/tiff] saved -> {os.path.abspath(out_path)}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print('  (open in ImageJ / FIJI / napari; mp4 codecs unavailable)')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    except Exception as e:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        print(f'tifffile path failed: {e}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"        out_path = None\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"if out_path is not None:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"    print(f'  {T_} frames, {Hs}x{Ws} px, {fps_play} fps')\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:02.714779699Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:01.633309223Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": null\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c08\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 2. Lazy zarr-native I/O\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Same as the clean tutorial — chunked time-axis, random-access, no full-movie loads. We persist the simulated movie to a zarr store the same way you would for a real recording.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c09\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:04.231009656Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:04.090085286Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"from cnmfe.io import save_zarr\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"tmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp()\\n\",\n",
    "    \"zarr_path = os.path.join(tmpdir, 'movie.zarr')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"z = save_zarr(movie, zarr_path, chunk_t=100)\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f'zarr shape  : {z.shape}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f'zarr chunks : {z.chunks}')\\n\",\n",
    "    \"print(f'frame[50]   : shape={z[50].shape}  (only that chunk was read)')\"\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"name\": \"stdout\",\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"stream\",\n",
    "     \"text\": [\n",
    "      \"zarr shape  : (500, 128, 128)\\n\",\n",
    "      \"zarr chunks : (100, 128, 128)\\n\",\n",
    "      \"frame[50]   : shape=(128, 128)  (only that chunk was read)\\n\"\n",
    "     ]\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   ],\n",
    "   \"execution_count\": 7\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c10\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"---\\n\",\n",
    "    \"## 3. Motion correction\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"Our synthetic movie has no rigid motion, but we still run the corrector so the rest of the pipeline matches the real-data path.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c11\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {\n",
    "    \"ExecuteTime\": {\n",
    "     \"end_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:07.211692426Z\",\n",
    "     \"start_time\": \"2026-05-27T15:09:05.687158556Z\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    "   },\n",
    "   \"source\": \"from cnmfe.motion_correction import motion_correction_rigid, estimate_shifts, apply_shift\\n\\n# Demo on one frame\\nframe = movie[50].copy()\\ntrue_drift = np.array([3.7, -2.1], dtype=np.float32)\\ndrifted = apply_shift(frame, true_drift)\\ncorrection = estimate_shifts(drifted, frame, upsample_factor=20)\\nprint(f'True drift          : {true_drift}')\\nprint(f'Detected correction : {correction}   (should equal -true_drift)')\\nprint(f'Subpixel error      : {np.abs(correction + true_drift).max():.3f} px')\\n\\n# Full-movie rigid correction (in-memory path: numpy in -> numpy out + shifts).\\n# gSig_filt high-pass is required for 1p data.\\nmc_movie, shifts = motion_correction_rigid(\\n    movie, max_shift=(15, 15), gSig_filt=7, upsample_factor=10, niter_rig=1, verbose=False\\n)\\nmc_movie = np.asarray(mc_movie, dtype=np.float32)\\n\\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 2.5))\\nax.plot(shifts[:, 0], label='dy'); ax.plot(shifts[:, 1], label='dx')\\nax.axhline(0, color='k', lw=0.5)\\nax.set_xlabel('Frame'); ax.set_ylabel('Shift (px)')\\nax.set_title(f'Per-frame shifts (max |shift| = {np.abs(shifts).max():.3f} px)')\\nax.legend(); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\",\n",
    "   \"outputs\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "     \"name\": \"stdout\",\n",
    "     \"output_type\": \"stream\",\n",
    "     \"text\": [\n",
    "      \"True drift          : [ 3.7 -2.1]\\n\",\n",
    "      \"Detected correction : [-3.85  2.05]   (should equal -true_drift)\\n\",\n",
    "      \"Subpixel error      : 0.150 px\\n\"\n",
    "     ]\n",
    "    },\n",
